# Run with: kanso play concat_bytes_interp_refuses.kso
#      and: kanso play concat_bytes_interp_refuses.kso --interp
#
# Should: print [97 98 0] on every engine -- text/bytes answers a list of
# ints, and text/concat appends two lists.
# Does: the native engines print [97 98 0]; the interpreter, which is meant
# to be the oracle, stops with `error[runtime]: concat takes two lists`.
# The same happens with both arguments from text/bytes, and with the list
# first. Found writing store/store_test.kso, where `kanso test` (which runs
# on the interpreter) failed a test the compiled program would have passed.
import "std/text"

print "{text/concat (text/bytes "ab") [0]}"
